# DMARC weekly check, for `crontab -e`.
#
# `dmarc-weekly.sh --crontab` prints this same line with the paths and values
# of the machine it is run on already filled in; this file is the version to
# read and adapt by hand.
#
# The run is silent when the week is clean, and mails the full output when
# something needs looking at or a report was filed by policy anyway.
#
# Three settings are not optional, because cron starts a job with almost no
# environment:
#
#   DMARC_EXPECTED  the address or addresses expected to send as these domains,
#                   separated by commas or spaces.  Without it the run stops
#                   with a usage error, which is deliberate: guessing would
#                   make every sender look expected.
#   MAIL_TO         where a week that needs looking at is mailed.
#   PERL            an absolute path to a perl that has XML::LibXML; cron's PATH
#                   usually holds only the system perl, which does not.
#
# DMARC_DIR, DMARC_ARCHIVE_DIR and the log location have defaults (~/Downloads,
# ~/dmarc-archive and ~/dmarc-report.log) and only need naming if they differ.

0 6 * * 1 DMARC_EXPECTED=192.0.2.10 MAIL_TO=alerts@example.org PERL=/usr/bin/perl /usr/local/bin/dmarc-weekly.sh
